{
    "sections": [
        {
            "body": "Three meals a day. Each meal has ten options — pick whichever one you feel like that day. They are all built to land within a few percent of each other, so any combination gives you the same day. Nothing here needs more than about fifteen minutes on a weekday.",
            "heading": "How to use this"
        },
        {
            "body": "About 1,800 calories and 90g protein a day, worked out from 135 kg at 165 cm, age 49, sedentary work. Matta rice rather than white wherever rice appears, and every plate has protein on it — that pairing is the part that matters most.",
            "heading": "Your numbers"
        },
        {
            "body": "Month one usually shows 4-5 kg. Two of that is fat and the rest is water your body lets go of in the first fortnight. Month two is normally around 2 kg, and that is the plan working properly rather than slowing down. Knowing that in week one is what stops week six feeling like failure.",
            "heading": "What to expect"
        },
        {
            "body": "Coconut is measured, not poured — roughly two tablespoons of grated coconut per curry, not a handful. That single habit is the difference between these numbers and half again as many calories. No deep-frying, and no cabbage or cauliflower, as you asked.",
            "heading": "Cooking notes"
        },
        {
            "body": "Every dish in this plan opens with a line telling you exactly how much fat goes into it, in both spoons and grams. Read that line before you start cooking, not after.\n\nAcross a whole day these add up to about 3 teaspoons of added oil — roughly 15g, or 133 calories. That is the number the plan is built on, and it is the single easiest one to break: a pour from the tin is 2 to 3 teaspoons by itself, so one poured curry can undo a day.\n\nThe three measures that matter, so a spoon means the same thing every time. One level teaspoon of oil is 5g and 45 calories. One level tablespoon of grated coconut is 10g and about 4g of fat. Thin second-extract coconut milk is roughly 5g of fat per 100ml, while the thick first extract is nearly four times that.\n\nMeasure the oil into a katori or a spoon first and cook from that, and stop when it is empty. Most of what you cannot see is coconut rather than oil: in avial, thoran and stew the grated coconut or the coconut milk carries more fat than the oil does, which is why each recipe names its coconut in grams too.",
            "heading": "How much oil, exactly"
        },
        {
            "body": "Take this plan, and your 27 May report, to your doctor before you begin. Your HbA1c and blood pressure are theirs to manage, and a calorie deficit is something they should know about. I will build around whatever they say.",
            "heading": "Before you start"
        }
    ],
